      "id": 1,
      "text": "Ellington was renamed in 1856 after Sina Huff Ellington, who let a railroad line cross her family’s farmland.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ellington%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Civil War Fortification at Barnesville, now part of Ellington, earned a spot on the National Register of Historic Places in 1998.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ellington%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Blue Spring, just outside Ellington, is Missouri’s deepest spring at 310 feet and ranks sixth in the state for size.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.islands.com/2023273/ellington-missouri-ozark-mountains-city-scenic-downtown-blue-spring/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A quick detour to the Reynolds County Museum lets you explore local history and access a research library open from March to October.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
